WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.350
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:11.006
Growing up, I desperately wanted to feel
a sense of connection and belonging.

00:00:11.006 --> 00:00:14.557
As an Asian American raised in New Canaan,
Connecticut,

00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:18.764
there were very few people of color and
even fewer who looked like me.

00:00:18.764 --> 00:00:22.637
And I didn't need to look myself in
the mirror to see that I was different.

00:00:22.637 --> 00:00:29.313
Instead my classmates reminded me of it,
every single day.

00:00:29.313 --> 00:00:32.852
They'd slant their eyes
on me in the hallway,

00:00:32.852 --> 00:00:37.843
instead of saying hello they greet
me by saying, konichiwa, and

00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:41.035
they call me nicknames like Jackie Chan.

00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:42.235
Now in most cases,

00:00:42.235 --> 00:00:46.812
these actions were delivered as jokes
rather than ill spirited racism.

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:49.080
But regardless, the message was clear.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.040
I didn't fit in.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.980
At first, I was frustrated and confused.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.690
I collected Pokemon cards
just like everyone else.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.653
I watched SpongeBob just
like everyone else.

00:01:02.653 --> 00:01:05.522
I thought I was American
just like everyone else.

00:01:08.923 --> 00:01:10.791
Eventually, frustration and

00:01:10.791 --> 00:01:15.036
confusion turned into darker feelings
of loneliness and self doubt.

00:01:15.036 --> 00:01:18.627
I'm not good enough, I told myself.

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:21.122
I'm not white enough.

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:22.759
I don't fit in.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.936
To combat these insecurities,
I began to shun my Asian nests.

00:01:28.936 --> 00:01:31.050
I ignored my Chinese teacher.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.990
I refuse to eat sushi or use chopsticks.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.230
I was even more excited to celebrate St.
Patrick's Day than Chinese New Year.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:39.900
kind of crazy.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.942
But at the end of the day,
I still looked Asian.

00:01:44.942 --> 00:01:48.108
And so the jokes still came.

00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:54.267
Humans have two basic but
powerful psychological needs.

00:01:54.267 --> 00:02:00.194
The first is to fit in,
the second is to stand out.

00:02:00.194 --> 00:02:05.543
We all want to feel a sense of belonging,
and yet, we all also want to feel special.

00:02:08.144 --> 00:02:14.730
A study conducted in 2019 found that over
60% of Americans report feeling lonely.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:19.551
Another survey found that 8 and
10 millennials believe,

00:02:19.551 --> 00:02:21.731
they aren't good enough.

00:02:21.731 --> 00:02:25.545
I felt disheartened hearing
these statistics, but

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:29.803
they also reminded me that I
am not alone in feeling alone.

00:02:32.621 --> 00:02:36.992
When my grandfather, who we call
Jaya immigrated from China in 1951.

00:02:36.992 --> 00:02:41.382
He arrived in the US with nothing and
with no one.

00:02:41.382 --> 00:02:48.350
He told my dad that he often felt hungry,
homesick, and alone.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.223
The language barrier made
it nearly impossible for

00:02:51.223 --> 00:02:53.370
him to connect with other Americans.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.499
But while he was pursuing his master's
at the University of Michigan,

00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:01.780
he began to develop
an interest in American sports.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.635
He then moved to New York City where that
interest evolved into a lifelong passion

00:03:05.635 --> 00:03:07.344
for several New York sports teams.

00:03:07.344 --> 00:03:11.970
As a kid, I remember him sporting
a grey New York Giants crewneck

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.896
sweatshirt anytime he came to visit us.

00:03:16.939 --> 00:03:21.890
It took me several years to fully grasp
the importance of Jaya's love for sports.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:27.102
But I now see how becoming
a sports fan helped him fit in.

00:03:27.102 --> 00:03:31.078
In 2004, my grandparents moved in with us.

00:03:31.078 --> 00:03:36.398
And for the next seven years, Jaya didn't
miss a single Michigan football game.

00:03:36.398 --> 00:03:42.585
He watched every single New York Giants,
Yankees, Rangers, and Knicks game.

00:03:42.585 --> 00:03:44.688
Why you might ask?

00:03:44.688 --> 00:03:48.787
Because they reminded him of
the happiest moments of his life.

00:03:48.787 --> 00:03:53.485
The times when he felt like an insider,
rather than an outsider.

00:03:53.485 --> 00:03:58.738
Sports with a common bond that helped
him to transcend differences and

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:03.736
connect with his classmates,
colleagues, and grandchildren.

00:04:03.736 --> 00:04:06.374
Today, I want to propose
a new solution for

00:04:06.374 --> 00:04:09.610
conquering feelings of loneliness and
self doubt.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.700
I want everyone here to consider sports
as a cure for the fear of disconnection.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.007
When we feel alone, what we need
most is connection with others.

00:04:21.007 --> 00:04:25.000
And sports have a unique
superpower that brings people

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.789
together regardless of background.

00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:30.604
Sports that outsiders and insiders.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.861
Take for example how becoming a sports fan
instantly connects you with the community.

00:04:36.861 --> 00:04:41.381
When we feel invested in a sport, or
team, or an athlete, we automatically feel

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:44.844
a sense of camaraderie with
others with similar affinities.

00:04:46.531 --> 00:04:51.243
Sports Psychologist Dr. Daniel Wan has
conducted multiple studies that have found

00:04:51.243 --> 00:04:56.022
statistically significant correlations
between sports team identification, and

00:04:56.022 --> 00:04:59.538
several measures of mental health,
Including self esteem and

00:04:59.538 --> 00:05:00.752
life satisfaction.

00:05:00.752 --> 00:05:03.199
And sports are accessible to anyone.

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:08.694
There are no prerequisites, no membership
fees, and no application processes.

00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:12.530
Sports also gives us a platform
to build deeper relationships.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.485
Research conducted by Professor Brian Uzi
at the Kellogg School of Management,

00:05:17.485 --> 00:05:20.900
found that the secret to building deep
relationships is shared experiences.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.540
And while there are many different types
of shared experiences, sports are unique

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.860
in their inherent combination of
socialness, levity, and authenticity.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:36.355
Consider the fact that 95 of the top
100 most watched TV programs last year,

00:05:36.355 --> 00:05:37.889
were sports telecasts.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.950
Or consider the fact that there are
thousands of sports bars around the world.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.440
But I have yet to come across
any movie bars or politics bars.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.140
Has anyone here ever
been to a weather bar?

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.460
Yeah, me neither.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.901
Sports are built in social events
that allow us to build and

00:05:56.901 --> 00:05:58.510
grow human connection.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.583
In May, 2012,

00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:05.190
I attended a college lacrosse game
that changed my life forever.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.525
On that day,
I met an incredible girl named Amy.

00:06:08.525 --> 00:06:13.429
Now on the surface Amy and
I had very little in common.

00:06:13.429 --> 00:06:16.333
Amy was a blonde haired,
blue eyed politics, and

00:06:16.333 --> 00:06:18.697
philosophy major from rural Virginia.

00:06:18.697 --> 00:06:21.525
Not how I describe myself.

00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:22.424
But on that day,

00:06:22.424 --> 00:06:26.747
sports allowed us to focus on the things
we shared rather than our differences.

00:06:28.662 --> 00:06:30.827
We shared our first meal together,

00:06:30.827 --> 00:06:34.445
a warm sugar coated funnel cake
from the concession stand.

00:06:34.445 --> 00:06:39.147
And we cheered on our classmates
from the bleachers together.

00:06:39.147 --> 00:06:43.228
10 years later, and
I can't wait to marry Amy this fall.

00:06:45.115 --> 00:06:48.911
Yeah, without sports, we may have
never realized the potential for

00:06:48.911 --> 00:06:51.208
a fulfilling, lifelong relationship.

00:06:53.687 --> 00:06:59.086
And finally, sports enable us
to embrace our differences.

00:06:59.086 --> 00:07:00.721
Consider Serena Williams,

00:07:00.721 --> 00:07:05.019
who dove head first into a sport lawn
associated with money and privilege.

00:07:07.672 --> 00:07:11.837
Serena's gone on to win 23
Grand Slam singles titles and

00:07:11.837 --> 00:07:17.119
is the only American, male or female,
to have won more than 20 majors.

00:07:17.119 --> 00:07:22.938
Serena has become one of
the greatest athletes of all time.

00:07:22.938 --> 00:07:26.166
In a recent interview Serena shared.

00:07:26.166 --> 00:07:30.375
I wouldn't be who I am,
I wouldn't be as strong as I am,

00:07:30.375 --> 00:07:34.672
I wouldn't have been able to be
as amazing as I have been so

00:07:34.672 --> 00:07:38.810
far in my career,
I wouldn't be me if I wasn't black.

00:07:44.113 --> 00:07:47.133
Being Asian in America is hard.

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:50.971
Being a minority in America is hard.

00:07:50.971 --> 00:07:58.922
You can't help but think about the things
that make you different, every single day.

00:07:58.922 --> 00:08:02.486
But as sports have helped me to
feel more connected to the world,

00:08:02.486 --> 00:08:05.131
I've been able to reframe
those differences.

00:08:05.131 --> 00:08:09.237
I wouldn't have had the same life
experiences, if I wasn't Asian.

00:08:09.237 --> 00:08:13.281
I wouldn't have the same view
of the world, if I wasn't Asian.

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:17.371
I wouldn't be standing here today,
if I wasn't Asian.

00:08:17.371 --> 00:08:21.739
If I wasn't Asian, I wouldn't be me.

00:08:21.739 --> 00:08:26.687
When sports helped me to fit in,
being Asian empowers me to stand out.

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:35.313
Jaya spent 60 years in America
before passing in 2011 after

00:08:35.313 --> 00:08:38.800
a 10 year battle with Parkinson's disease.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.768
But his love for
sports lives deep in the hearts of my dad,

00:08:42.768 --> 00:08:45.675
my three younger brothers, and myself.

00:08:45.675 --> 00:08:51.127
He taught us a universal language,
the language of sports, that has helped us

00:08:51.127 --> 00:08:57.441
to build bridges to our American
side of being Asian American I'm

00:08:57.441 --> 00:09:01.788
16 years old playing running back from
my high school's varsity football team.

00:09:01.788 --> 00:09:07.178
As I jog out of the locker room and onto
the field, I no longer see slanted eyes.

00:09:07.178 --> 00:09:11.010
No one says konichiwa,
no one calls me Jackie Chan.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.352
Instead, I'm greeted with high fives and
chest bumps.

00:09:16.352 --> 00:09:19.761
As I joined my teammates
in the huddle on the field,

00:09:19.761 --> 00:09:24.537
the quarterback Jimmy simply turns
to me and says, let's go brother.

00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:33.136
Embracing sports is the single most
important thing I've done for myself.

00:09:33.136 --> 00:09:37.650
It's been my cheat code for fostering
connection and combating loneliness.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:44.136
Here at the GSB, I have built
valuable friendships on the soccer

00:09:44.136 --> 00:09:49.527
field at Maples Pavilion, and
at Old Pro, the sports bar downtown.

00:09:49.527 --> 00:09:54.626
By giving me a home where I fit in,
sports have given me the courage

00:09:54.626 --> 00:10:00.949
to embrace my differences and appreciate
how special it is to be Asian American.

00:10:00.949 --> 00:10:04.631
I'm Asian, and I'm American.

00:10:04.631 --> 00:10:07.520
I fit in, and I stand out.

00:10:10.574 --> 00:10:14.817
So the next time you feel alone,
or like you're not good enough,

00:10:14.817 --> 00:10:18.611
find a sport, or team, or
an athlete that interests you.

00:10:18.611 --> 00:10:22.350
It could be one of Stanford's
36 varsity sports teams.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:23.940
Or it could be your daughter's
little league team.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.181
And then go watch a game.

00:10:27.181 --> 00:10:30.483
You never know who you might meet.

00:10:30.483 --> 00:10:31.640
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:36.342
[MUSIC]

